  "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef optim_classical_momentum(X: list, y: list, lr: float = 0.05, momentum: float = 0.9, num_steps: int = 10) -> dict:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Classical Momentum** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef optim_classical_momentum(X: list, y: list, lr: float = 0.05, momentum: float = 0.9, num_steps: int = 10) -> dict:\n    Xa, ya = np.array(X, dtype=np.float64), np.array(y, dtype=np.float64)\n    N, D = Xa.shape; w, v, losses = np.zeros(D), np.zeros(D), []\n    for _ in range(int(num_steps)):\n        losses.append(round(float(np.mean((np.dot(Xa, w) - ya)**2)), 6))\n        g = (2.0/N) * np.dot(Xa.T, np.dot(Xa, w) - ya)\n        v = float(momentum)*v + g; w -= float(lr)*v\n    return {'final_weights': np.round(w, 4).tolist(), 'loss_curve': losses}\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
